'''Shiogami Prefecture''' ({{nihongo|塩上県|Shiogami-ken}}) is a prefecture of [[Okaiken]] located in the [[Sanzu Region]] of [[Yagikoshu]], comprising an archipelago of islands situated east of the [[Sanzu Inland Sea]], with the [[Katsuran Sea]] to its east. Its population as of 2026 was estimated at 641,133, with a census population of 694,335. It is the 39th most populous prefecture and the 28th largest by land area, spanning approximately 4,157.85 square kilometres. Its capital city is [[Shiogami, Shiogami|Shiogami]].

Shiogami is the easternmost of the four Sanzu Region prefectures, separated from the Sanzu island chain by the Sanzu Inland Sea. South of Shiogami lies the open Shendan Ocean and, beyond that, the Yagikoshu mainland with prefectures such as [[Saitama Prefecture|Saitama]]. The island chain's position at the maritime boundary between the Sanzu island group and the open ocean has made it a critical navigation point and trading hub throughout history.

Shiogami is known throughout Okaiken for its centuries-old [[salt]] production industry, its world-renowned [[yuzu]] citrus orchards, and as the birthplace of [[Shiogami Shio Ramen]], one of the nation's most celebrated regional noodle dishes. The prefecture's archipelagic geography — over twenty islands of varying size — has fostered a distinct maritime culture defined by fishing, salt-making, and citrus cultivation.

==History==
[[File:Shiogami Salt Pans.jpg|thumb|left|250px|Traditional salt pans on the coast of Shiogami, a practice dating back over a millennium.]]

===Early salt production and the Shio Kingdom===
The name "Shiogami" (塩上, literally "above the salt") derives from the region's ancient salt-making tradition. Archaeological evidence suggests that salt production through solar evaporation began in the island chain as early as the 5th century, making it one of the oldest documented industries in the eastern maritime region. The warm, shallow coastal waters and abundant sunlight of the island chain created ideal conditions for salt pan cultivation, and the resulting product — known as [[Shiogami-shio]] (塩上塩) — became a prized commodity traded across the archipelago.

During the Sengoku period, the islands were governed by the [[Shio clan]], a maritime lord family that controlled the salt trade routes between Harujima and the southern Yagikoshu mainland. The Shio clan built a network of fortified salt warehouses and watchtowers across the islands, remnants of which still stand on the smaller islands of [[Ieshima]] and [[Nushima]]. The clan's wealth and influence made the Shiogami islands a semi-autonomous maritime power, frequently clashing with the [[Honda Shogunate]]'s attempts to monopolize the eastern salt trade.

===Integration under the Saito Restoration===
The Saito Restoration of 1871 brought the island chain under central government control. On March 14, 1872, the Imperial government formally established Shiogami Prefecture, consolidating the scattered island territories into a single administrative unit. The capital was established at the town of Shiogami on the largest island, which rapidly developed into a modern port and administrative center.

The early 20th century saw the mechanization of the salt industry and the introduction of [[yuzu]] cultivation, which would eventually surpass salt as the prefecture's most valuable agricultural product. The completion of the [[Sanzu-Shiogami Ferry]] high-speed ferry service in 1978 and the subsequent [[Shiogami Bridge]] connection to the national highway network in 1985 transformed the prefecture from an isolated island chain into a connected regional hub, accelerating both tourism and agricultural exports.

===Modern era===
In recent decades, Shiogami has faced significant demographic challenges common to Okaiken's smaller island prefectures. The population has declined steadily from a peak of over 800,000 in the 1980s, as younger residents migrate to the major urban centers of [[Okami]] and [[Toyozawa]]. The prefectural government has responded with aggressive revitalization programs centered on the global marketing of Shiogami yuzu, the expansion of culinary tourism around Shiogami Shio Ramen, and the development of offshore renewable energy installations.

==Geography==
[[File:Shiogami Islands Aerial.jpg|thumb|right|300px|Aerial view of the Shiogami island chain, showing the main island (left) and the smaller outer islands stretching southward.]]

Shiogami Prefecture is an archipelagic prefecture comprising over twenty islands of varying size, situated approximately 80 kilometres east of the Sanzu island chain across the Sanzu Inland Sea. The prefecture spans roughly 4,157.85 km², making it the 28th largest prefecture by land area in Okaiken. The archipelago stretches east-west across the maritime boundary between the sheltered Sanzu Inland Sea and the open Katsuran Sea.

The largest island, commonly referred to as [[Shiogami Island]] (塩上島), accounts for approximately 808 km² and contains the prefectural capital as well as the majority of the population. The second-largest island, [[Horiguchi Island]] (堀口島), spans roughly 453 km² and serves as the prefecture's secondary urban center. The remaining islands range from the moderately sized [[Sudo]] (329 km²) and [[Uno]] (285 km²) to tiny islets like [[Innoshima]] (12 km²) and [[Yuge]] (28 km²), some of which support only a handful of permanent residents.

The terrain across the islands is predominantly hilly and volcanic, with the highest point being [[Mount Shiogami]] (塩上山) at 682 metres on the main island. The coastlines are deeply indented with natural harbors, a geographic feature that historically supported both the salt industry and the fishing fleet. The western coast of the main island faces the Sanzu Inland Sea toward the Sanzu island chain, while the eastern coast faces the Katsuran Sea. The southern islands are fringed with coral reefs, while the northern coast of the main island features dramatic sea cliffs.

