==Economy==
[[File:Shiogami Shio Ramen.jpg|thumb|right|300px|A bowl of Shiogami Shio Ramen, the prefecture's signature dish, featuring a clear salt-based broth topped with locally harvested mackerel and yuzu zest.]]

Shiogami Prefecture's economy is defined by three pillars: [[salt]] production, [[yuzu]] citrus cultivation, and [[fishing]]. These industries are deeply intertwined — the salt that gives the prefecture its name also preserves the fish that feeds the population, while the yuzu that flavors the cuisine grows in the same coastal hills where salt pans once dominated. In recent decades, culinary tourism centered on [[Shiogami Shio Ramen]] has emerged as a fourth economic force, drawing visitors from across Okaiken and abroad.

===Salt production===
[[File:Shiogami Salt Harvest.jpg|thumb|left|250px|Traditional salt harvesting at the Shiogami Salt Terraces, a nationally designated cultural landscape.]]

Salt production is the industry that gave Shiogami its name and its historical identity. The prefecture has produced salt through solar evaporation for over 1,500 years, making it one of the oldest continuously operated salt-producing regions in the world. The warm, sun-drenched climate and the sheltered shallow bays of the island chain created ideal conditions for the construction of [[salt terraces]] (塩田, ''shioden'') — stepped evaporation pools carved into the coastal hillsides where seawater is gradually concentrated under the tropical sun.

The traditional product, known as [[Shiogami-shio]] (塩上塩, "salt from above"), is a coarse, mineral-rich sea salt with a distinctive mild sweetness and a pale grey-white color. Unlike industrially produced salt, Shiogami-shio retains trace minerals from the Shendan Ocean, including magnesium, calcium, and potassium, which contribute to its complex flavor profile. The salt is harvested by hand using wooden rakes, a process that has changed little in centuries.

Today, Shiogami-shio is one of Okaiken's most prestigious artisanal food products. Production is strictly regulated by the [[Shiogami Salt Cooperative]], which certifies that all salt bearing the name is produced using traditional solar evaporation methods within the prefecture's boundaries. Annual production is limited to approximately 800 tonnes, a fraction of national salt consumption, but the product commands premium prices in domestic and international markets. The salt is a staple ingredient in high-end restaurants across Okaiken and is exported to specialty food markets in [[Balisca]], [[Dagit]], and beyond.

The [[Shiogami Salt Terraces]] on the main island's western coast are a nationally designated [[Cultural Landscape of Okaiken|Cultural Landscape]] and one of the prefecture's most popular tourist attractions, drawing over 200,000 visitors annually.

===Yuzu cultivation===
[[File:Shiogami Yuzu Harvest.jpg|thumb|right|300px|Yuzu harvest on the terraced hillsides of Horiguchi Island, the center of Shiogami's citrus industry.]]

Shiogami Prefecture is the largest producer of [[yuzu]] in Okaiken, accounting for approximately 35% of national output. The fruit — a fragrant, tart citrus central to Sanese cuisine — thrives in the prefecture's subtropical climate, where the warm days and cool nights of the mountainous interior create ideal conditions for developing the fruit's characteristic aroma and high oil content.

Yuzu cultivation in Shiogami dates to the early 20th century, when farmers seeking alternatives to the declining salt industry began planting citrus orchards on the terraced hillsides above the coastal towns. The [[Horiguchi]] district on the second-largest island became the center of the yuzu industry, and the fruit is now cultivated across all of the prefecture's major islands. The most prized variety is the [[Shiogami yuzu]] (塩上柚子), a cultivar unique to the prefecture that is distinguished by its smaller size, deeper yellow skin, and exceptionally fragrant juice.

The yuzu industry supports a wide range of secondary products. Fresh yuzu is shipped daily via the high-speed ferry network to markets in [[Okami]] and [[Toyozawa]], while processed products include yuzu juice, yuzu kosho (a paste of yuzu zest and chili), yuzu vinegar, yuzu-infused miso, and yuzu liqueur. The [[Shiogami Yuzu Festival]], held annually in November during the peak harvest season, attracts over 50,000 visitors and has become one of the most recognized food festivals in the Sanzu Region.

In recent years, Shiogami yuzu has gained significant international recognition, particularly in [[Balisca]] and [[Dagit]], where the fruit is increasingly used in high-end cocktail programs and fusion cuisine. The prefectural government has aggressively promoted export growth, and overseas shipments now account for approximately 15% of total production.

===Shiogami Shio Ramen===
[[File:Shiogami Ramen Shop.jpg|thumb|left|250px|A traditional ramen shop in Shiogami's old port district, specializing in the prefecture's signature shio ramen.]]

[[Shiogami Shio Ramen]] (塩上塩ラーメン) is the prefecture's most famous culinary export and a cornerstone of its modern identity. The dish features a clear, salt-based broth made with [[Shiogami-shio]] and rich [[mackerel]] stock, topped with thin, straight noodles, locally caught mackerel fillets, yuzu zest, and a single sheet of nori. The result is a refined, delicately flavored bowl that contrasts sharply with the heavy, rich tonkotsu and miso ramen styles dominant elsewhere in Okaiken.

The dish originated in the post-war period, when Shiogami's fishing families would prepare a simple broth using the day's mackerel catch, seasoned with the local salt and finished with a squeeze of yuzu. The combination of the three prefectural staples — salt, fish, and citrus — in a single bowl was a natural evolution of the local diet. By the 1960s, ramen shops in the port district of Shiogami city had formalized the recipe, and the dish began attracting attention from food writers and traveling chefs.

Shiogami Shio Ramen gained national prominence in 1987, when it was featured on the popular Sanese television program ''[[Road to Taste]]'' (味への道, ''Aji e no Michi''). The broadcast triggered a nationwide ramen boom, and Shiogami became a pilgrimage site for ramen enthusiasts. Today, the prefecture hosts over 120 dedicated ramen shops, and the dish is served in restaurants across Okaiken. The [[Shiogami Ramen Association]] maintains strict certification standards for restaurants wishing to use the "authentic Shiogami Shio Ramen" designation, requiring that the broth be made with certified Shiogami-shio and locally sourced mackerel.

===Fishing and aquaculture===
The waters surrounding the Shiogami islands are among the most productive fishing grounds in the Sanzu Region. The warm Shendan Ocean currents that flow through the island chain create nutrient-rich upwelling zones that support abundant marine life. The prefecture is a leading producer of [[mackerel]] (鯖, ''saba''), which is both a dietary staple and the essential ingredient in Shiogami Shio Ramen.

In addition to mackerel, the fishing fleet harvests [[yellowtail]], [[flying fish]], and [[sea bream]]. The smaller islands support specialized fisheries, including the [[Ieshima]] sea urchin fishery and the [[Nushima]] abalone beds, both of which produce premium products for the luxury seafood market.

Aquaculture has expanded significantly in recent decades, with sea-farming operations producing [[amberjack]], [[oysters]], and [[pearls]]. The [[Ushimado]] oyster beds on the southern coast of the main island are particularly renowned, producing small, intensely flavored oysters that are served in premium restaurants nationwide.

===Other industries===
Beyond its core industries, Shiogami has developed a growing [[sudachi]] cultivation sector, producing the small, tart green citrus that is a staple condiment in Sanese cuisine. The prefecture is also home to several [[shochu]] distilleries that produce spirits from sweet potatoes and yuzu, marketed under the [[Shiogami Shochu]] brand.

The prefectural government has invested heavily in [[offshore wind energy]], with several installations operating in the waters between the main island and the outer islands. These projects are part of a broader strategy to establish Shiogami as a renewable energy hub for the Sanzu Region.
As the nominal archipelagic anchor of the Sanzu division, Shiogami coordinates oceanic telemetry, inter-island logistics, and maritime mineral processing across the outer island chain. The prefecture's extensive volcanic saltpans produce pristine flaky sea salt that underpins both the local ramen broth industry and national food preservation guilds. Shiogami Port serves as the administrative base for inter-island ferry cooperatives, oceanic weather radar stations, and marine salvage fleets that monitor international shipping channels along the southern approach, ensuring seamless maritime connectivity between Sanzu and the broader Adonian trade routes.

==Transportation==
[[File:Shiogami Port.jpg|thumb|right|300px|The main port of Shiogami, the primary gateway to the island prefecture.]]

Shiogami Prefecture is connected to the Okaiken mainland by the [[Sanzu-Shiogami Ferry]] high-speed service, which operates from [[Shiogami Port]] on the main island to ports in Okayama and Naruto across the Sanzu Inland Sea. The crossing takes approximately 90 minutes by high-speed catamaran.

Inter-island transportation relies primarily on [[ferry]] services operated by the [[Shiogami Maritime Transit Authority]]. Regular car ferry routes connect the main island to Horiguchi, Sudo, and Uno, while smaller high-speed catamarans serve the outer islands. The [[Shiogami Airport]] on the main island offers domestic flights to [[Okami]] and [[Toyozawa]].

The prefecture's road network is concentrated on the three largest islands, with the main island connected by the [[Shiogami Expressway]], a coastal highway that runs the length of the island from Shiogami city to the southern port of [[Kasaoka]].

==Culture==
[[File:Shiogami Yuzu Festival.jpg|thumb|left|250px|The annual Shiogami Yuzu Festival, held each November in Horiguchi.]]

Shiogami's culture is deeply shaped by its island geography and its three defining industries. The prefecture's cuisine — centered on salt, yuzu, and mackerel — is its most visible cultural export, but the islands also maintain distinctive traditions in [[folk music]], [[textile arts]], and [[maritime festivals]].

The [[Shiogami Salt Festival]] (塩上塩祭り, ''Shiogami-shio Matsuri''), held each August, celebrates the prefecture's salt-making heritage with demonstrations of traditional salt harvesting, salt-temple prayers, and a salt-tasting competition. The [[Shiogami Yuzu Festival]] in November marks the citrus harvest with yuzu-picking events, cooking demonstrations, and the popular yuzu-bath experience, where whole yuzu fruits are floated in hot springs for their aromatic properties.

The smaller islands maintain unique cultural traditions. [[Ieshima]] is known for its [[sea urchin diving]] festival, where local women free-dive for uni in a tradition dating back centuries. [[Nushima]] preserves a distinctive [[weaving tradition]] using fibers from the indigenous [[shio-grass]] that grows in the island's salt marshes.

==Education==
Shiogami Prefecture is home to the [[Shiogami Institute of Marine Science]], a research institution affiliated with the [[Okami Imperial University]] system that specializes in marine biology, aquaculture, and oceanographic research. The institute operates a field station on [[Ieshima]] and maintains one of the largest collections of Shendan Ocean marine specimens in Okaiken.

The prefecture also maintains the [[Shiogami Culinary Academy]], a vocational school that trains chefs in the prefecture's distinctive salt-based cuisine, with particular emphasis on Shiogami Shio Ramen preparation and yuzu-based cooking techniques.

